13/02/2026 12:56:27 - Lee Booth (Work notes)
[code]
<h1><strong>Investigation Steps – Batch Printing Issue (Case CS1729850)</strong></h1>
<p><strong>Scenario Summary (from case notes)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>GP letters printed nightly (\~300).</li>
<li>Prints \~20 letters, then Symphony crashes.</li>
<li>After recovery, prints 3 letters at a time with long pauses (\~5 minutes).</li>
<li>Another user later reported low?memory warnings and a full PC crash.</li>
<li>Issue appears tied to <strong>one specific PC</strong> (GUH ED CEAU PC asset 0089729).</li>
<li>Error logs from that PC show nothing relevant.</li>
<li>Queue contained a large number of attendances (273 from previous day).</li>
<li>Possible deleted attendance in queue flagged by support.</li>
<li>MIDAS errors also observed on that PC around the same time.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h1><strong>1. Confirm the Printing Environment</strong></h1>
<p>From the case, this issue is happening on <strong>one specific PC</strong>. Before further investigation:</p>
<ol>
<li>Verify:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Exact workstation name (PC asset 0089729).</li>
<li>Whether this PC is <strong>dedicated</strong> for batch printing.</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>Ask the site:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Is batch printing done <strong>only from this PC</strong>?</li>
<li>Have they tried printing from <strong>another PC</strong>?</li>
</ul>
<p>If another PC works fine, the issue is workstation-specific (memory, AV, printer drivers, corrupt temp folders).</p>
<hr>
<h1><strong>2. Validate the Batch Print Queue</strong></h1>
<p>Case data shows large backlog (273 attendances + the new day’s attendances).  <br>Slow printing + crashes can occur if:</p>
<ul>
<li>The queue contains <strong>a deleted or invalid attendance</strong>.</li>
<li>A <strong>single corrupted GP letter</strong> or malformed template is included.</li>
<li>The PC is attempting to open too many template instances at once.</li>
</ul>
<p>Steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Identify the GP Letter print job ID for the site.</li>
</ol>
<ol>
<li>Query print jobs for the specific date range and check:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Are there <strong>deleted</strong> or <strong>merged</strong> attendances in the queue?</li>
<li>Are any entries assigned to <strong>invalid patient IDs</strong>?</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>Remove or manually clear any invalid queue items.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h1><strong>3. Check GP Letter Print Job Configuration</strong></h1>
<p>Based on typical configuration issues and case hints:</p>
<ol>
<li>Verify:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Print Template path for GP letters (not on a disconnected drive).</li>
<li>&quot;Use Document Template&quot; enabled if using newer print templates.</li>
<li>&quot;Batch Print&quot; option enabled.</li>
<li>The job is not configured to &quot;Print to Printer AND File&quot; unnecessarily.</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>Confirm the GP letter template:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Opens normally in Symphony.</li>
<li>Has not been modified recently.</li>
<li>Does not contain invalid fields or embedded images.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the template is corrupted, <strong>only certain letters will cause the crash</strong>, consistent with the behaviour observed.</p>
<hr>
<h1><strong>4. Test Batch Printing Manually</strong></h1>
<p>On a test workstation (not the problematic one):</p>
<ol>
<li>Run batch printing for:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>A small date range (5–10 attendances).</li>
<li>The full date range (if safe to do so).</li>
</ul>
<p>If the issue <strong>does not</strong> occur on another PC ? workstation fault.  <br>If the issue <strong>does</strong> occur elsewhere ? data/template problem.</p>
<hr>
<h1><strong>5. Workstation-Specific Checks (very likely relevant)</strong></h1>
<p>The user saw <strong>low-memory errors</strong> and complete PC crash. This strongly suggests workstation-level resource exhaustion. Perform:</p>
<h3><strong>5.1 Restart the PC</strong></h3>
<p>Ensure the restart fully clears memory + temp directories.</p>
<h3><strong>5.2 Check free RAM and storage</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li>PC may not have enough memory for generating 300+ GP letters.</li>
<li>Confirm:</li>
<li>At least 8GB RAM available.</li>
<li>Adequate free disk space (&gt;10 GB recommended).</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>5.3 Anti-Virus</strong></h3>
<p>AV scanning of print temp files can slow batch printing drastically.</p>
<ul>
<li>Add AV exclusions for Symphony directories.</li>
<li>Exclude folder used for:</li>
<li>Print Temp</li>
<li>Document Template paths</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>5.4 Check Printer Driver</strong></h3>
<p>A failing or outdated driver can cause:</p>
<ul>
<li>Silent crashes</li>
<li>Delayed “print spooler” behaviour (printing a few, then pausing)</li>
</ul>
<p>Ensure:</p>
<ul>
<li>Correct departmental printer installed.</li>
<li>No duplicate printers.</li>
<li>Printer reachable over network.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h1><strong>6. Investigate MIDAS Errors</strong></h1>
<p>Case notes indicate “lots of MIDAS errors” around the same time from the same PC.</p>
<p>Potential impact:</p>
<ul>
<li>MIDAS services competing for RAM/CPU.</li>
<li>Failing scanner or imaging component affecting overall workstation performance.</li>
<li>Interruptions in DEP-triggered operations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Actions:</p>
<ol>
<li>Confirm whether MIDAS is actively used on this PC.</li>
<li>Update/remove problematic scanner drivers.</li>
<li>Re-test printing afterwards.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h1><strong>7. Enable Diagnostic Logging</strong></h1>
<p>If still unresolved, enable:</p>
<ul>
<li>Print Logs</li>
<li>Document History Logs (if available for their version)</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask the site to:</p>
<ol>
<li>Enable logging on the workstation.</li>
<li>Attempt the batch print again.</li>
<li>Identify the attendance number where slowdown/crash begins.</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h1><strong>8. Determine Whether It Is Data-Specific</strong></h1>
<p>Ask the site to test:</p>
<ol>
<li>Print only:</li>
</ol>
<ul>
<li>Yesterday’s attendances.</li>
<li>Today’s attendances.</li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li>Print letters for specific departments only.</li>
</ol>
<p>If a specific attendance or day always triggers the crash:</p>
<ul>
<li>That GP letter is likely corrupted or contains problematic data.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h1><strong>9. Provide Next Steps to the Site</strong></h1>
<p>Here is a clean message you can send to the customer:</p>
<hr>
<h3><strong>Customer-Facing Message You Can Paste</strong></h3>
<p>Hi Christopher,</p>
<p>Thanks for the update and the detail provided.</p>
<p>We are investigating the batch printing issue affecting GP letters on workstation PC 0089729. We’ve noted the low?memory warnings, the machine crash, and the slow “3 letters every 5 minutes” behaviour.</p>
<p>To progress the investigation, please could you confirm the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>Is batch printing always done from this same PC?</li>
<li>Are you able to test printing the GP letters from a different workstation tonight?</li>
<li>Can you confirm the exact GP Letter print job name used?</li>
<li>If possible, could you provide the attendance number where the printing last stopped or slowed down?</li>
</ol>
<p>We suspect either a workstation-specific resource issue or a problematic attendance/template within the batch. Confirming the above will help us narrow this down.</p>
<p>Kind regards,  <br>Lee</p>
<hr>
<h1><strong>Summary</strong></h1>
<p>Based on the evidence, the <strong>most likely causes</strong> for this incident are:</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>workstation fault</strong> (low memory, MIDAS errors, failing printer driver).</li>
<li>A <strong>corrupt attendance</strong> in the print queue.</li>
<li>A <strong>GP letter template issue</strong> affecting specific records.</li>
</ul>
<p>This investigation plan addresses all three and aligns to the case detail.</p>
[/code]

